Agent team bus: joining a team as a `podman` (ccy) member
=========================================================

A `podman` member (`--type=podman`) is a Claude Code agent in a ccy session on the same
machine as the team's homeserver. This file is the short version; the full guide,
including what joining a team grants and its limits, is docs/agent-bus.md in the
fedora-desktop repository.

A ccy session does not use this kit: `pingbus`, the plugin and the settings come from the
ccy image, and ccy's entrypoint adds them only to a session launched with `--teams`.

1. Launching into a team. A ccy session is in a team only when it is launched with it:

   ```
   ccy --teams <seat>@<team>[,<seat>@<team>...]
   ```

   One seat per team per session; a session may be in several teams. A plain `ccy` is in
   no team, whatever seats the checkout has. Nothing in the checkout opts it in: nothing
   about the bus goes in ccy.env.local.

2. Seats. A seat is a durable member of one team in this checkout: `dev1@<team>` is the
   same member, with its handle, role, pending pings and history, every time a session is
   launched into it. The first launch that names a seat creates it on the host, through
   one `add-member` run with sudo (an interactive launch lets sudo ask for the password; a
   headless one uses only sudo's cached credential, so run `sudo -v` first), and writes
   its bundle to .claude/ccy/pingbus/seats/<team>/<seat>/, which ccy's
   .claude/ccy/.gitignore keeps out of git. One live session holds a seat at a time; a
   launch into a held seat is refused. A session taking over a seat reads what the seat
   received and sent before with `pingbus history`.

3. The handle. A seat's handle is `<repo>.<seat>+<host>.podman`: `<repo>` from the
   checkout's forge remote, `<host>` the HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME that the checkout's
   ccy.env.local assigns, or the literal `local` when it assigns none. A handle never
   carries the machine's hostname: handles appear in public forge text.

4. On the host, in the checkout, as yourself (not through sudo):

   ```
   agent-bus seat list
   agent-bus seat remove <seat>@<team>
   ```

   `seat list` prints a SEAT line per seat (team, seat, held or free, `self` or `-`,
   handle). `seat remove` parks the seat's handle in its team (its token revoked; its
   account, role and history kept) and deletes its directory, and the checkout's bus
   directory once nothing is left in it; launching into the same `<seat>@<team>` again
   returns the seat with a new token. A seat whose directory was lost is recovered the
   same way: remove it, then launch again. To give a seat another role:
   `sudo agent-bus set-role <team> <handle> --role=orchestrator`, the handle read from
   `agent-bus seat list`.

The team's homeserver must run on this machine (agent-bus-hs@<team>.service is active),
and the seat reaches it at the machine's bus address, on agentbus0.
